  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 138 AM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Gusty winds and cool temperatures end our week \nbefore 60s return Saturday.\n\nA vigorous upper-level trough presently across the southern \nAppalachians and lower OH valley will track through the Mid-Atlantic \nstates today before exiting off Maine tonight. In its wake, quasi-\nzonal flow takes over for Saturday. \n\nAt the surface, an occluded low over the Lower Great Lakes will \ncontinue to bring gusty winds across central NC due to the continued \ntight pressure gradient and 35-40 kt winds in the lowest 5 kft. The \nwinds are expected to be strongest into mid-morning, at which time \nsurface winds of 30 to 35 mph will be common. This afternoon, it \nwill remain gusty, though wind speeds aloft decrease to around 25-30 \nkt, supportive of 25-30 mph wind gusts and infrequent 35 mph gusts. \nThese wind gusts will finally start to relax tonight as the system \npulls away and the gradient relaxes. Even still, gusts to 20 mph \nwill still be around through the late evening. These gusts are shy \nof wind advisory criteria, although could still result in some minor \ndowned tree limbs and blow around unsecured objects.\n\nIt will be a cool end to the week with low to middle 50s. Tonight, \nwe drop down into the upper 30s to around 40, close to average for \nthis time of year owing to the mixed boundary layer. Warmer weather \nis in store for Saturday with temperatures mainly near the lower 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Turning warmer this weekend, followed by a period \nof showers Sunday evening and Sunday night.\n\nSlightly above normal temperatures will return to central NC this \nweekend with deep SW flow ahead of the next mid-latitude cyclone \nthat tracks NE from the Southern Plains NE into the MS Valley on \nSaturday night/Sunday, then into the OH Valley, Lower Great Lakes \nand Northern New England Sunday night/Monday. The associated \nnorthern-stream shortwave will quickly swing ENE across the TN/OH \nValleys on Sunday evening then into the Mid-Atlantic on Sunday \nnight, perhaps becoming negatively-tilted. Forecast highs are in the \nupper 50s to lower 60s on Saturday and lower to mid 60s on Sunday. \n\nModerate to strong mid-level height falls will overspread central NC \non Sunday evening/night as the aforementioned shortwave approaches, \nlikely aiding in the development of a band of showers along and \nahead of a cold front. Models continue to have good consensus on the \nstrength and track of the low/shortwave, though the GFS is a bit \nslower compared to the ECMWF and CMC. PW values will briefly be 125-\n175% of normal during this period, but rainfall amounts should not \nbe too heavy due to a lack of instability and the progressive nature \nof the system. ECMWF/GFS/CMC ensemble means generally depict around \na tenth to a third of an inch, highest NW and lowest SE, with \ndeterministic guidance closer to a quarter to half an inch. A warm \nfront will lift north through the area on Sunday evening, providing \na brief window of dew points in the 50s before the cold front moves \nthrough. Low and mid level wind fields are certainly impressive with \nthe GFS and ECMWF depicting a 50-60 kt jet streak at 925 mb and 90-\n100 kts at 500 mb. Will need to watch the potential for any deeper \nconvection bringing any of these winds to the surface, but \nconfidence in this occurring is low given a lack of instability and \npoor low-level lapse rates. Even outside of any showers, S/SW wind \ngusts of 20-30 mph will be possible from Sunday afternoon into \nSunday night.\n\nAny rain chances will come to an end by early Monday morning. \nTemperatures should drop back to near normal on Monday and Tuesday \nas surface high pressure builds in from the TN and OH Valleys. \nMonday and Tuesday nights look chilly with good radiational cooling \npotential and forecast lows in the upper-20s to mid-30s.",
